<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Project DefaultTargets="Build" ToolsVersion="4.0" xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/developer/msbuild/2003">
  <!-- Settings shared by the Windows client components - the ACM codec and the
       DirectShow filter - which the rest of the tree gets from elsewhere.

       C4100, unreferenced formal parameter: the library suppresses this for
       MSVC in libmp3lame/machine.h, with the reasoning that it is intentional
       in stub and compatibility entry points and that the GCC/Clang
       -Wunused-parameter build is where a genuinely unused parameter is caught
       (see LAME_UNUSED).  That reasoning holds here for the same shape of code
       - debug-output stubs whose parameters go unreferenced exactly when debug
       output is compiled out, and window procedures that must accept the full
       message signature - but machine.h is an internal library header, so a
       C++ client of the public API never sees it.  Hence the same policy,
       stated in the one place all three client projects share.

       Deliberate narrowing conversions are NOT suppressed here.  The library
       disables C4244 wholesale; the clients have few enough of them to cast at
       the site and say what bounds the value, which is the more useful thing
       to leave behind. -->
  <ItemDefinitionGroup>
    <ClCompile>
      <DisableSpecificWarnings>4100;%(DisableSpecificWarnings)</DisableSpecificWarnings>
    </ClCompile>
  </ItemDefinitionGroup>
</Project>
